FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/logging.rs:127 — Timestamps are produced with `chrono::Utc::now()` directly instead of the host clock the rest of the runtime uses (`rb_set_clock` / `runtime::set_host_clock`); on `wasm32-unknown-unknown` `SystemTime::now()` has no clock and panics (src/wasm.rs:877-886 explicitly says so and exists to avoid exactly this), so an enabled logging call in the playground aborts the run rather than emitting a record, and even where a JS clock exists the timestamp disagrees with `time.now()` on a host that set the clock. — Fix: derive the timestamp from the shared host-clock accessor used by `time.now()` (runtime.rs:520), falling back to `Utc::now()` only on native targets. — VERIFIED: traced src/logging.rs:127 to src/wasm.rs:877-886 ("`wasm32-unknown-unknown` has no clock of its own and `SystemTime::now()` panics there"); not exercised on a wasm target.

2. [MAJOR] src/wasm.rs:31 — `CAPTURING`/`CAPTURED` are process-global while the new logging configuration is thread-local (src/logging.rs:70-72), and `logging.rs:171` consults the global `is_capturing()` without taking `RUN_LOCK`; a `stdout` log call on one host thread therefore appends its record into whichever other thread currently has a capture open, corrupting that run's output and losing the record. The REPORT's claim that thread-local configuration "isolates tests" is only true for threshold/destination, not for the capture sink. — Fix: make the capture state thread-local, or route the logging `stdout` path through the same serialization (`run_lock`) as `emit`. — Not verified by running; a test that runs a capture on one thread while another thread logs to `stdout` would settle it.

3. [MINOR] src/logging.rs:154 — `libc::write` returning `-1` is treated as a hard failure without checking `EINTR`; a signal can turn a valid log write into a spurious catchable `IoError`. — Fix: retry when `io::Error::last_os_error().kind() == ErrorKind::Interrupted`.

4. [MINOR] tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs:1862 — The only "closed stream" fixture is a read-only file (`EBADF`); there is no test for the REPORT's explicit claim that "a pipe with no reader raises the same catchable `IoError`" (src/logging.rs:140-145 relies on this). A pipe whose reader has exited exercises `EPIPE`, a different errno and a different signal path. — Fix: add a test writing to a pipe with a closed read end and assert the `Io` catch fires.

5. [MINOR] SPEC.md:1231 — The new logging example is classified RUNS (`tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt`) but calls `logging.set_destination("app.log")`; executing the block writes/append-accumulates `app.log` in the executor's working directory instead of a scratch path, polluting the checkout across runs. — Fix: point the example at a temp path, use `stdout`, or classify it as a fragment; a temp-CWD executor would settle whether this is already harmless.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 5
